<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Church Fathers</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Early Church Fathers, Fathers of the Church, Christian Fathers</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Church Fathers were ancient Christian theologians and writers who laid the intellectual and doctrinal foundations of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>. Their age, the Patristic Era, ran roughly from the late 1st to the mid-8th century and flourished in the 4th and 5th centuries, as Christianity became the state church of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>. The earliest, the Apostolic Fathers such as Clement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, Ignatius of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> and Polycarp of Smyrna, were believed to have known or been shaped by the apostles. Many denominations count their writings as part of Sacred Tradition, though some, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/origen">Origen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tertullian">Tertullian</a>, had parts of their teaching later condemned.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/church-fathers.jpg" alt="Church Fathers" width="330" height="279" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anonymous Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Otsy.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The writers of the early church, the <b>Church Fathers</b>, shaped its doctrine: Irenaeus, Origen and Tertullian in the 2nd and 3rd centuries; Athanasius, the Cappadocian Fathers, John Chrysostom, Ambrose, Jerome and Augustine of Hippo in the 4th and 5th.</blockquote><a href="/religion/christianity">Christianity · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>Until 1945 Gnosticism was known mainly from the <b>Church Fathers</b> who opposed it, and their writings had an antagonistic bias and were incomplete.</blockquote><a href="/religion/gnosticism">Gnosticism · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>From the 2nd century CE the <b>Church Fathers</b> condemned the empire’s non-Christian religions as “pagan”.</blockquote><a href="/religion/roman-religion">Roman religion · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>Biographers, novelists, satirists and historians, such as Plutarch, Lucian and Polybius, continued the tradition, and the New Testament and the works of the <b>Church Fathers</b> made Greek the language of Christian thought.</blockquote><a href="/language/ancient-greek">Ancient Greek · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>There were sermons and saints’ lives, biblical translations, the works of the early <b>Church Fathers</b>, laws and wills, and books on grammar, medicine and geography.</blockquote><a href="/language/old-english">Old English · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>Humanist scholars called for a return to the sources of Christianity, the Bible in its original languages and the early <b>Church Fathers</b>; in 1516 Erasmus published the Greek New Testament with notes correcting the Latin Vulgate.</blockquote><a href="/movement/protestant-reformation">Protestant Reformation · Background</a></li></ul></section>
